      "title": "Pair complementary angles",
      "body": "<p>The terms at angles <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B8;</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>90</mn><mo>&#x02218;</mo></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003B8;</mi></mrow></math> are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>sin</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>&#x003B8;</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>cos</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>&#x003B8;</mi></mrow></math>, whose sum is 1.</p>",
      "recap": "Complementary-angle identities can turn a trigonometric sum into equal-sized groups.",
      "pitfall": "Count both endpoint terms; if you pair within one copy, account separately for any unpaired middle term.",
      "pause": "Use the complementary-angle identity to group the terms, then count and add the groups carefully.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l16",
          "reminder": "Complementary-angle identities can turn a trigonometric sum into equal-sized groups."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "solution": "<p>Write the sum once forwards and once backwards. In their sum, each pair is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>sin</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>&#x003B8;</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msup><mi>sin</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>90</mn><mo>&#x02218;</mo></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003B8;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>sin</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>&#x003B8;</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msup><mi>cos</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>&#x003B8;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p>There are 91 such pairs, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>91</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>45.5</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p><strong>Answer E.</strong></p>",
